<% founder_quote = founder_quote() %>
<.auth_layout title={founder_quote} compact>
  <:headline>
    <% founder_name = "Stefan Wintermeyer" %>
    <%!-- Founder hero: the whole quote in one heading at a single size, then a
          one-line signature. On a phone everything in this panel is set
          smaller and tighter than from `md` up, so the teaser video and the
          top of the sign-up form both fit on the first screen. --%>
    <div>
      <h1 class="text-xl font-bold leading-tight md:text-3xl">
        <.quote_sentences text={founder_quote} />
      </h1>
      <%!-- The link comes from `:landing_example_profile_url`, the key whose own
            comment names this page — hardcoding it sent a third-party
            installation's start page to a profile on vutuv.de, and left the
            line standing for an operator who had cleared the key (the 404
            page already honoured it). An installation that cleared it gets
            the name as plain text rather than a link into somebody else's
            site. The *name* stays written here: it is an attribution, not a
            setting. --%>
      <p class="mt-1 text-right text-xs text-brand-100 md:mt-2 md:text-sm">
        <a
          :if={example_profile_url()}
          href={example_profile_url()}
          class="font-semibold text-white underline decoration-brand-200 underline-offset-4 hover:decoration-white"
        >
          {founder_name}
        </a>
        <span :if={is_nil(example_profile_url())} class="font-semibold text-white">
          {founder_name}
        </span>
        <span aria-hidden="true">·</span> {gettext("Founder of vutuv")}
      </p>
      <%!-- The one sentence saying what vutuv is. The quote alone says only
            that it is like LinkedIn without the annoyance, which tells a
            visitor who never used LinkedIn nothing. The kinds in the
            parenthesis are prose, not a join over Organization.kinds/0:
            German gives every noun its own case ending. --%>
      <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-snug text-brand-100 md:mt-6 md:text-base">
        {gettext(
          "A business network for people and organizations (businesses, public authorities, associations, universities …)."
        )}
      </p>
    </div>
  </:headline>
  <:hero>
    <%!-- What the quote provokes, answered in three lines: somebody who agrees
          that LinkedIn is annoying wants to know what they lose by leaving, what
          they have to retype, and what it will cost them later. The strings
          live in `hero_points/1`. --%>
    <.hero_points />
    <%!-- The teaser: a silent film of one member's morning on vutuv. It plays
          only on a click, so the page still loads nothing heavy. --%>
    <.teaser_video :if={Teaser.enabled?()} class="mt-4 md:mt-6" />
  </:hero>
  <%!-- The sign-up form is three steps now (name and address, the settings, the
        topics), so it holds state between them and is a LiveView
        (`VutuvWeb.RegistrationLive`). It is embedded rather than routed for the
        reason the profile is: this controller keeps owning the page, and
        everything below the card stays the plain HTML a crawler reads.

        The CSRF token is handed over because the last step submits an ordinary
        `POST /new_registration` — the token has to be the one this request's
        session carries. It is no secret; every rendered form on the page shows
        the same value.

        `form_state` is set only by the rejected-submit path, which is what lets
        the wizard come back on its last step with the errors on it instead of
        dropping the member back to an empty first step. --%>
  {live_render(@conn, VutuvWeb.RegistrationLive,
    id: "registration-live",
    session:
      Map.merge(VutuvWeb.ControllerHelpers.live_render_session(@conn), %{
        "csrf_token" => Plug.CSRFProtection.get_csrf_token(),
        "form_state" => @form_state
      })
  )}
</.auth_layout>

<%!-- Everything below the sign-up card answers the questions a visitor has
      before signing up, and nothing else (the words and the reasoning live
      in `landing_faq/0`; the FAQPage block at the end is built from the same
      list). It sits under the form on purpose: the form is the conversion
      path and keeps the top of the page. Same max-w-5xl as <.auth_layout> so
      every edge on the page lines up. All questions stay open: a collapsed
      block reads as something to hide.

      What this replaced: six promise tiles under two headings and a "for the
      technically minded" section (418 words, two screens), and before those
      seven sections with four screenshot decks (landing_page_test says what
      must not come back). --%>
<% faq = landing_faq() %>
<section data-landing-faq class="mx-auto max-w-5xl pb-12">
  <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 md:text-3xl dark:text-white">
    {gettext("Frequently asked questions")}
  </h2>
  <%!-- gap-x-16: with the usual gap-x-8 the two columns read as one text at
        the page's full width (Stefan, 2026-09-25). --%>
  <div class="mt-6 grid gap-x-16 gap-y-6 md:grid-cols-2">
    <.faq_entry :for={entry <- faq} entry={entry} />
  </div>
</section>

<JsonLd.script data={JsonLd.organization()} />
<JsonLd.script data={JsonLd.web_site()} />
<JsonLd.script data={JsonLd.faq_page(faq)} />
